# No lazy deferral

Work scoped to a PR lands in that PR. A deferral is a decision with a
required form. It is not the default when a commit grew, an item is
tedious, or a consumer "can land later."

## The rule

A deferral of scoped work is legitimate only when all three hold:

1. **A named blocking reason exists.** "Blocked on X" where X is a
   concrete, checkable dependency: an unlanded harness, an external
   standard, a gated upstream PR, a review decision not yet made. "The
   commit was getting big" is a split reason: a split stays inside the
   same PR. "It felt separable" is neither a split nor a deferral.
2. **The deferral is tagged, and its owner resolves.** A
   `docs/FOLLOWUPS.md` entry (or an amendment to the PR's own plan) names
   the blocker, a `Target:` (`95-documentation-lifecycle.mdc`), the
   reopening criterion (`21-reversion-clause-discipline.mdc`), and an
   **`Owner:`** that resolves to a live `docs/design/` document or a
   registered identifier family. Gated by `check_followups_owners.py`.
   An owner is a doc, a family, or a plan slot. A person's name, a lane
   name, or a PR number is not an owner. An allow-comment pointing at a
   future consumer is a residue marker, not the deferral record.
3. **The deferral is disclosed where it was made.** The commit message
   or the review report for the commit that sheds the work states what
   was scoped, what is deferred, why, and where it lands. A deferral
   found later by audit, rather than disclosed by the commit that made
   it, is a process failure even when the deferral would have been approved.

Silent deferrals accumulate a queue nobody decided. The decision belongs
to the reviewer, on disclosure.

## Callee without caller

A PR that lands a callee whose production caller is "a future commit" has
deferred integration. Inert-until-wired is legitimate when the plan names
the commits and the consumer. It is a silent deferral when the caller was
scoped here and became "later" without a plan change. The tell is an allow
comment whose named consumer shifted between commits.

## Name the falsifier

A blocker is named when the entry says how a reader would discover it had
lifted. Write *"blocked on X — falsify by `<check>`"*.

A falsifier is a test, a command whose output would change, a PR whose
merge lifts the block, or a named decision. It is not a restatement of the
blocker. *"Blocked until the builder exists"* is the claim, not a check.

It is also not a **state** some other change could reach. "The manifests
are at `format_version: 3`" goes green when any writer bumps that number.
A **field-shaped** falsifier — "every manifest carries this injection's
`height` and `persona`" — can be satisfied only by the change that
produces those fields. Name the fields the work produces, not the number
the work would also happen to move.

If no falsifier can be written, the item is not deferred. Nobody knows
what would unblock it. That is closer to REJECTED-with-reasoning
(`23-disposition-visibility.mdc`) than to DEFERRED: a permanent rejection
wearing a conditional's clothes.

An unfalsifiable threshold eventually gets contradicted. An unfalsifiable
blocker never does. The parked work just sits.

- State the condition so it can be observed to have **failed**. *"Skip if
  X lands first"* has no failure reading. *"Skip if X lands first; if X
  is still open when this file is next touched, the skip is void"* does.
- A deferral is not discharged by time. When you touch a file that carries
  one, re-check the condition before you inherit the conclusion.
- Do not read a block off a neighbouring sentence. "Deleting A is blocked"
  is not "A cannot run." Check what the blocked item's subject is.

## Two deferrals that pass the form and were still unchosen

**The blocker is itself a deferral.** A register row that excludes a
deletion as "retires with item N" is a chain when item N is ruled or
deferred and not scheduled. Ask what the item is blocked on in code. If
the answer is nothing, it is deletable now.

**A parity artifact outlives the rule it pins.** A retirement ruling that
names the mechanism and not the mirrors, KATs, fixtures, fuzz targets, and
equivalence tests leaves those green after the rule is gone. A green gate
whose subject has vanished is rule 47. The retirement names the artifacts
that die with it. Once the code stops doing the thing, the FOLLOWUPS row
is the only remaining statement of what is owed.

